.\" btrdasd(1) man page
.\" Generated from clap CLI definitions in indexer/src/main.rs
.TH BTRDASD 1 "2026-10-03" "0.7.22.3" "DAS Backup Manager"
.SH NAME
btrdasd \- DAS Backup Manager indexer and orchestrator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B btrdasd
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B btrdasd
(ButteredDASD) manages BTRFS backups to Direct-Attached Storage (DAS).
It provides btrbk orchestration, content indexing with FTS5 full-text search,
health monitoring, schedule management, scheduled BTRFS scrub, automatic
adoption and retirement of subvolumes, subvolume drift detection, and backup
history tracking.
.PP
Progress messages are printed to stderr so that stdout remains
machine-parseable.
When the global
.B \-\-json
flag is given, all read commands emit JSON output on stdout.
.PP
Commands that read or write the backup targets
.RB ( walk ,
.BR reindex ,
.BR reconcile ,
.BR forget ,
.BR purge ,
.B backup
and
.BR restore )
mount the targets themselves, after taking the DAS maintenance lock (see
.BR FILES ),
and unmount what they mounted afterwards. A
target is found by its
.B mount_uuid
(a filesystem with that UUID, as
.B "blkid \-U"
reports it) or by one of its drive serials, and is mounted as
.BI UUID= uuid
when
.B mount_uuid
is set. A busy mount point is unmounted again up to five times, two seconds
apart; one still mounted after that fails the command with an error that reads
.B "still mounted:"
followed by the paths.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS walk
Index all new snapshots on a backup target.
.PP
.B btrdasd walk
.I TARGET
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-wait ]
.TP
.I TARGET
Path to backup target mount point.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
Do not wait for the DAS maintenance lock: while another job holds it, print
.B Deferred
with the holder's name and exit 75, having mounted nothing (with
.BR \-\-json :
.BR {"deferred":true,"holder":"..."} ).
.PP
The targets are mounted for the walk, the same pass as
.B btrdasd reconcile
runs while they are still mounted, and they are unmounted afterwards.
.PP
Before it mounts anything,
.B walk
takes the DAS maintenance lock
.RI ( /run/das\-maintenance.lock ).
While a backup, scrub, restore or other job holds it,
.B walk
prints one line naming the holder, as the holder recorded itself in the lock
file, and how to stop a scrub that cannot be waited for (stop and mask
.BR das\-scrub.service ,
unmask it afterwards), and then waits for the lock. A recovery drive lent to a
virtual machine is held this way for the whole session: mounting its
filesystem on the host as well would corrupt it.
.B backup\-run.sh
runs
.B walk
while it already holds the lock, and hands its hold down through
.B DAS_MAINTENANCE_LOCK_FD
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
A command whose own caller holds the lock, and did not hand it down, fails at
once, naming that caller, instead of waiting for it forever.
.\"
.SS search
Full-text search across indexed files using FTS5.
.PP
.B btrdasd search
.I QUERY
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-limit
.IR N ]
.TP
.I QUERY
FTS5 search query.
Supports prefix matching (e.g.,
.BR report* ).
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Maximum number of results to return.
Default: 50.
.\"
.SS list
List files in a specific snapshot.
.PP
.B btrdasd list
.I SNAPSHOT
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SNAPSHOT
Snapshot path or name.timestamp pattern.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.\"
.SS info
Show database statistics (snapshot count, file count, span count, database size).
.PP
.B btrdasd info
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.\"
.SS reconcile
Remove index rows for snapshots that no longer exist on disk.
.PP
Backup targets are unmounted between runs, so an absent path is normally no
evidence at all that a snapshot is gone. A snapshot is therefore only ever a
prune candidate when its target root is a
.B verified mountpoint;
anything under an unmounted or unrecognised root is skipped and counted, never
pruned. Spans that merely touch a pruned snapshot have their endpoints moved
inward to the nearest surviving snapshot of the same series rather than being
deleted, so a file is never dropped from snapshots that still exist.
.PP
Targets are mounted for the duration of the pass and unmounted afterward.
.B btrdasd reconcile
takes the maintenance interlock without waiting: while a backup, scrub or
other maintenance pass holds it, the command prints
.B Deferred
and exits 0, changing nothing.
.B btrdasd walk
also runs the prune automatically while targets are still mounted, under the
maintenance lock
.B walk
holds; it waits for that lock rather than deferring (see
.BR walk ).
.PP
.B btrdasd reconcile
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-forget\-root
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-repair ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used for target mount points.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be removed without changing anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-forget\-root " PATH"
First drop every index row recorded under
.IR PATH ,
a mount path that is no longer a configured target (one retired by a rename,
for example). Such rows can never be reconciled, because that root is never
mounted again. A
.I PATH
that is a configured target is refused, and nothing else is done.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
First delete spans whose endpoints name snapshots that do not exist, and the
files left with no span. Needed on an index carrying such rows, because foreign
keys are enforced and they cannot be rewritten. Has no effect with
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.\"
.SS reindex
Re-index every mounted backup target.
.PP
With
.B \-\-rebuild
the existing index is discarded first and rebuilt from scratch. This is the only
repair for two defects that cannot be fixed in place, because both are properties
of rows already written: spans whose endpoints name snapshots that no longer
exist (foreign keys are enforced, so such rows cannot be rewritten at all), and
file rows migrated from schema v1, whose subvolume series cannot be recovered
after the fact. Backup run history and target usage history are preserved \(em
they are operational records, not derived content.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-rebuild
this is an incremental top-up across every mounted target, indexing only
snapshots not already present.
.PP
The targets are mounted for the duration and unmounted afterwards. Like
.BR reconcile ,
it defers (prints
.BR Deferred ,
exits 0) while the maintenance interlock is held.
.PP
.B btrdasd reindex
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-rebuild ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used for target mount points.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
Discard the index and rebuild it. Takes hours on a large target set.
.\"
.SS forget
Delete obsolete snapshots whose series name matches a pattern.
.PP
For snapshots stranded by a subvolume rename: the old
.I snapshot_name
matches no retention rule, so btrbk never prunes them \(em
.B btrbk clean
handles incomplete and garbled snapshots only, and
.B btrbk prune
matches configured names \(em and they accumulate indefinitely.
.PP
.B Refuses outright
if the pattern also matches a series btrbk is still backing up. The live series
are read from
.IR /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf ,
the file btrbk itself uses, rather than re-derived from
.IR config.toml ,
so the guard cannot disagree with reality. Without that file the command exits
with status 2 rather than proceeding unguarded. A refusal, or a pattern that
matches nothing, exits 1.
.PP
The snapshots are found through the index and deleted with
.BR "btrfs subvolume delete" ;
their index rows are then pruned. The targets are mounted for the duration,
and the command defers (prints
.BR Deferred ,
exits 0) while the maintenance interlock is held.
.PP
.B btrdasd forget
.I PATTERN
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-btrbk\-conf
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I PATTERN
Glob over the snapshot series name
.RI ( * / ? ),
for example
.IR Projects\-old\-name .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan without deleting anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-btrbk\-conf " PATH"
btrbk configuration read for the series still being backed up.
Default:
.IR /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf .
.\"
.SS purge
Delete every snapshot containing files matching a path pattern.
.PP
.B A file cannot be removed from inside a backup.
Target snapshots are received read-only subvolumes; deleting a file requires
clearing
.IR ro ,
which permanently destroys the Received UUID that only
.B btrfs receive
can set, leaving a subvolume that looks like a valid replica while its content
may drift. Whole-snapshot deletion is therefore the only sound granularity, and
purge deletes whole snapshots \(em losing those restore points, and forcing a
full re-send of the affected series on the next backup.
.PP
Unlike
.BR forget ,
purge deliberately has no live-series guard: removing a leaked credential means
removing it from the series still being backed up, which is the case it exists
for.
.PP
.B btrdasd purge
.I PATH_PATTERN
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I PATH_PATTERN
SQLite
.B GLOB
over the file path within a snapshot (case-sensitive), for example
.IR *id_rsa .
Only files the index knows about are matched.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan without deleting anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS setup
Interactive setup wizard \(em configure backup sources, targets, and scheduling.
Requires root privileges.
.PP
.B btrdasd setup
.RB [ \-\-modify ]
.RB [ \-\-upgrade ]
.RB [ \-\-uninstall ]
.RB [ \-\-uninstall\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.TP
.B \-\-modify
Re-open the wizard with the current configuration pre-filled.
.TP
.B \-\-upgrade
Regenerate all managed files from the existing configuration
(useful after a binary update).
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall
Remove all generated files, disable timers, and optionally remove the database.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall\-all
Remove ALL installed files: generated configs (same as \-\-uninstall), plus
cmake-installed binaries (btrdasd, btrdasd\-gui, btrdasd\-helper), the backup
scripts under
.IR /usr/lib/das\-backup ,
D\-Bus configs, polkit policy, systemd units, man page, shell completions,
desktop entry, icon, and the FFI library and header that releases up to
0.7.22.1 installed. Prompts whether to remove the backup database.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Non-interactive mode: skip all prompts and never remove or overwrite the
backup database. Enables unattended installs, upgrades, uninstalls, and
full uninstalls. For install without an existing config, returns an error
(use the interactive wizard first). Useful for scripted deployments and
CI/CD pipelines.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Validate the configuration and dependencies, report issues,
but change nothing. It reports whether the configuration is valid, whether the
mail relay is reachable (when email is enabled), whether every generated file is present, and, for each
attached target, whether udev actually hides its drives from udisks (read back
from
.BR "udevadm info \-\-export\-db" ,
not from the rule file; an exposed target is reported with the fix). It exits 0
whatever it finds, unless the configuration cannot be loaded at all. Takes
precedence over every other flag. A target with no
.B mount_uuid
is reported, with the UUID udev knows for its drive when it is attached and
the line to add to its
.B [[target]]
\(em without one, the CLI and GUI find the target by drive serial only and
verify only that its mount path is a mount point. The setup wizard records
.B mount_uuid
for a new target whose drive is attached;
.B \-\-upgrade
never adds it to an existing config.
.\"
.SS config dump\-env
Print shell-sourceable KEY=VALUE pairs from the configuration.
.PP
.B btrdasd config dump\-env
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS config show
Pretty-print the current configuration.
.PP
.B btrdasd config show
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS config validate
Validate the configuration and report any issues.
Exits with status 1 if validation errors are found.
.PP
.B btrdasd config validate
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS config edit
Open the configuration file in
.BR $EDITOR
(defaults to
.BR vi ).
.PP
.B btrdasd config edit
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS backup run
Run the full backup pipeline: sync subvolumes, snapshot, send, boot archive, index, and email report.
Before btrbk runs, the same sync step as
.B "btrdasd subvol sync"
adopts new subvolumes and retires vanished ones, and the run then uses the
updated config.toml. A backup started from the GUI runs the same step. A sync
failure does not stop the run; the run goes on with what is already configured,
is recorded as failed in the backup history and the report (which carries the
.B "SUBVOLUME SYNC"
section), and exits non-zero at the end. A target or source volume the run
mounted and cannot unmount again (each is tried five times, two seconds
apart) fails the run the same way: the error reads
.BR "still mounted:"
followed by the paths. Once the targets are mounted and verified, the run
creates the directory each one receives into (the first
.I target_subdirs
entry of each source, or its label), logging each one it creates, on a dry
run too; a directory that cannot be created fails the run. This command does not
expire retired backups
.RB ( backup\-run.sh
does, with
.BR "subvol expire" ).
When
.B [boot].enabled
is set, every run of this command, incremental or full, archives the boot
subvolumes on each non-mirror target and replaces them from the latest
snapshot;
.B backup\-run.sh
replaces an existing one only on a full run.
.PP
If another backup already holds the singleton lock, the command prints that it
declines and exits 0; it waits for a running scrub. A run that could not start
(locks, mounting, target verification, btrbk) exits 1 and is not recorded. A run
that started and failed is recorded and reported, then exits 1.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup run
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-full ]
.RB [ \-\-sources
.IR LABELS ]
.RB [ \-\-targets
.IR LABELS ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Preview: takes the locks, mounts and verifies the sources and targets, and runs
the sync as
.BR "subvol sync \-\-dry\-run" ,
but creates, sends, archives and indexes nothing, and neither reports nor
records the run. A missing target directory is still created and logged, as
above.
.TP
.B \-\-full
Run a full backup instead of incremental:
.B "btrbk run"
in place of
.B "btrbk snapshot"
followed by
.BR "btrbk resume" .
Both apply the retention policy.
.TP
.BI \-\-sources " LABELS"
Comma-separated source labels to back up.
Default: every source with at least one subvolume that is not manual-only.
.TP
.BI \-\-targets " LABELS"
Comma-separated target labels to send to.
Default: all mounted targets.
.\"
.SS backup snapshot
Create snapshots without sending them to targets
.RB ( "btrbk snapshot" ).
Like the other manual
.B backup
commands below, it declines (exit 0) while another backup holds the singleton
lock, and waits for a running scrub.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup snapshot
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-sources
.IR LABELS ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-sources " LABELS"
Comma-separated source labels.
Default: all.
.\"
.SS backup send
Send existing snapshots to targets
.RB ( "btrbk resume" ,
which also applies the retention policy).
.PP
.B btrdasd backup send
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-targets
.IR LABELS ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-targets " LABELS"
Comma-separated target labels.
Default: all mounted.
.\"
.SS backup boot\-archive
Archive boot subvolumes on backup targets: on each non-mirror target, each
subvolume in
.B [boot].subvolumes
is snapshotted read-only to
.IB name .archive. timestamp
and then replaced from its latest btrbk snapshot. Mirror targets, which carry
their own operating system, are skipped. Snapshot names are read from
.IR /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf ;
if it cannot be read, nothing is done.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup boot\-archive
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS backup report
Show the last backup reports from the database.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup report
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-limit
.IR N ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Number of recent runs to show.
Default: 10.
.\"
.SS backup record\-run
Record a completed backup run in the backup history. Used by
.BR backup\-run.sh ;
not normally run by hand.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup record\-run
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-success ]
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR MODE ]
.RB [ \-\-snaps\-created
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-snaps\-sent
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-bytes\-sent
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-duration\-secs
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-errors
.IR TEXT ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.B \-\-success
Record the run as successful; without it the run is recorded as failed.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
.B incremental
or
.BR full .
Default:
.BR incremental .
.TP
.BI \-\-snaps\-created " N"
Number of snapshots created. Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-snaps\-sent " N"
Number of snapshots sent to targets. Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-bytes\-sent " N"
Bytes sent to targets. Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-duration\-secs " N"
Duration in seconds. Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-errors " TEXT"
Error messages, one per line. Default: none.
.\"
.SS restore file
Restore specific files from a snapshot.
.PP
Every
.B restore
command mounts the targets for its duration, after taking the DAS maintenance
lock exactly as
.B walk
does: it waits for the lock, saying who holds it, or with
.B \-\-no\-wait
exits 75 at once. For
.B restore file
and
.BR "restore snapshot" ,
the snapshot must lie under a target's mount point, and the destination
must lie under one of
.B [restore].allowed_roots
in config.toml (default
.I /home
and
.IR /tmp ),
and never under a system path such as
.IR /etc ,
.IR /usr ,
.IR /boot ,
.I /var/lib
or the served roots
.I /srv/http
and
.IR /srv/ftp ,
which no configuration can allow. Both are compared after symlinks are
resolved, before any directory is created; files are written without following
symlinks, and a member path that is absolute or contains a
.I .\&
or
.I ..\&
component is rejected.
.PP
.B btrdasd restore file
.I SNAPSHOT DEST FILE
.RI [ FILE .\|.\|.]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-wait ]
.TP
.I SNAPSHOT
Path to the snapshot directory.
.TP
.I DEST
Destination directory for restored files.
.TP
.I FILE
One or more file paths relative to the snapshot root (at least one required).
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets and for
.BR [restore].allowed_roots .
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
As for
.BR walk .
.\"
.SS restore snapshot
Restore an entire snapshot using btrfs send/receive or recursive copy.
.PP
.B btrdasd restore snapshot
.I SNAPSHOT DEST
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-wait ]
.TP
.I SNAPSHOT
Path to the snapshot directory.
.TP
.I DEST
Destination directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
As for
.BR "restore file" .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
As for
.BR walk .
.\"
.SS restore browse
Browse files in a snapshot directory.
.PP
.B btrdasd restore browse
.I SNAPSHOT
.RB [ \-\-prefix
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-wait ]
.TP
.I SNAPSHOT
Path to the snapshot directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " PATH"
Optional subdirectory prefix to browse within the snapshot.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
As for
.BR walk .
.\"
.SS schedule show
Show the current backup schedule, including next run times.
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule show
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS schedule set
Update schedule settings in config.toml. Only config.toml is written; the
timer units change at the next
.BR "btrdasd setup \-\-upgrade" .
This command does not validate what it writes: check the result with
.B "btrdasd config validate"
before the next backup run.
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule set
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-incremental
.IR TIME ]
.RB [ \-\-full
.IR SCHEDULE ]
.RB [ \-\-delay
.IR MINUTES ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-incremental " TIME"
Incremental backup time in HH:MM format.
.TP
.BI \-\-full " SCHEDULE"
Full backup schedule as
.RI \(dq Day " HH:MM" \(dq,
where
.I Day
is one of
.BR Sun ,
.BR Mon ,
.BR Tue ,
.BR Wed ,
.BR Thu ,
.B Fri
or
.B Sat
(e.g., "Sun 04:00"); a bare
.I HH:MM
means Sunday. A systemd calendar expression such as "Sun *\-*\-* 04:00:00" is
not accepted by validation.
.TP
.BI \-\-delay " MINUTES"
Randomized delay in minutes before starting
.RB ( [schedule].randomized_delay_min ).
.\"
.SS schedule enable
Enable scheduled backups:
.B "systemctl enable \-\-now"
for
.I das\-backup.timer
and
.I das\-backup\-full.timer
(with OpenRC,
.BR "rc\-update add das\-backup" ;
with SysV init, the generated cron entry). The scrub and drift-check timers
are not affected.
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule enable
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS schedule disable
Disable scheduled backups (the same two timers, with
.BR "systemctl disable \-\-now" ).
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule disable
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS schedule next
Show the next scheduled backup time.
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule next
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol list
List all configured subvolumes across all sources. Retired entries are listed
too, and are not marked as retired;
.B "btrdasd config show"
shows their
.B retired
date.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol list
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol add
Add a subvolume to a source. Validates the whole configuration, then saves
config.toml and regenerates
.I /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf
together (each replaced atomically), or changes neither file.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol add
.I SOURCE NAME
.RB [ \-\-manual\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SOURCE
Source label to add the subvolume to.
.TP
.I NAME
Subvolume name (e.g., "@home").
.TP
.B \-\-manual\-only
Mark the subvolume as manual-only, excluding it from automatic backups.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol remove
Remove a subvolume from a source. Validates and saves config.toml and
regenerates btrbk.conf together, as
.BR "subvol add" .
.PP
Removing the entry of a subvolume that still exists does not stop it being
backed up: the next
.B "subvol sync"
(every backup run) adopts it again. To leave a subvolume out, add a pattern
to
.B [subvolumes].exclude
instead. The snapshots the removed entry already made are not expired by
.BR "subvol expire" ,
which only handles retired entries.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol remove
.I SOURCE NAME
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SOURCE
Source label.
.TP
.I NAME
Subvolume name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol set\-manual
Mark a subvolume as manual-only, excluding it from automatic backups.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol set\-manual
.I SOURCE NAME
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SOURCE
Source label.
.TP
.I NAME
Subvolume name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol set\-auto
Mark a subvolume for automatic backups, removing the manual-only flag.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol set\-auto
.I SOURCE NAME
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SOURCE
Source label.
.TP
.I NAME
Subvolume name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol sync
Adopt new subvolumes and retire vanished ones. Every
.B backup\-run.sh
run calls this after verifying the source volumes and before btrbk, and
.B "btrdasd backup run"
calls it too, so creating a subvolume is enough to have it backed up.
.PP
Each subvolume that exists on a source volume and is not excluded gets a
.I [[source.subvolumes]]
entry. A nested subvolume joins its nearest configured ancestor's source and
takes that source's targets. One with no configured ancestor goes to the
primary target only, through a per\-volume
.I <source>\-adopted
source. An adopted entry records the date in
.IR adopted .
An entry whose subvolume is gone is marked
.I retired
with the current date (UTC) and leaves btrbk.conf (see
.BR "subvol expire" ).
A retired entry whose subvolume returns is revived.
Sync mounts nothing: the source volumes must already be mounted, as
.B backup\-run.sh
and
.B "btrdasd backup run"
arrange.
.PP
Excluded are: anything under a
.I .snapshots
or
.I .btrbk\-snapshots
directory, the built\-in
.B @tmp
and
.BR @var\-tmp ,
and the patterns in
.I [subvolumes].exclude
(the older
.I [doctor].exclude
is still read and merged). A pattern
.RI ( * / ? ;
.I *
also matches
.IR / )
names the subvolume
.B without
a trailing slash and also covers everything nested under it:
.I @cache
excludes
.I @cache/x
as well, while
.I @cache/
excludes nothing.
.PP
A volume is read only if every device its sources name is a real mountpoint
holding the expected filesystem UUID, mounted at its top level. Otherwise
nothing on that volume is adopted or retired, and the run is marked failed.
config.toml and btrbk.conf are replaced together or not at all.
.PP
.I /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf
is generated. A sync that changes config.toml writes both files together, even
if some volume could not be read. A sync with nothing to change rewrites it
whenever it differs from what config.toml renders to, and says so in its
report, except on a dry run or when the sync failed (a volume was not read):
then the file is left alone and the report says it is out of date. So do every configuration change made from the GUI,
.BR "subvol add" ,
.BR remove ,
.B set\-manual
and
.BR set\-auto ,
and
.BR "btrdasd setup \-\-upgrade" .
A hand edit is lost the next time any of them rewrites it; change config.toml
instead.
.PP
An adopted entry that is nested under a configured subvolume uses that
subvolume's source: its targets, snapshot directory and target subdirectory,
and its manual\-only flag. One with no configured ancestor goes to the volume's
.I <first\-source\-label>\-adopted
source, created on first need, which takes its device and snapshot directory
from the first source on that volume, uses its own label as the target
subdirectory, and sends to the primary target only. Each adopted entry gets an
explicit snapshot name, the name btrbk would have derived made unique across
the whole configuration with
.IR \-2 ,
.IR \-3 ,
and so on; existing names never change. Under
.B backup\-run.sh \-\-dryrun
this step runs as
.B "subvol sync \-\-dry\-run \-\-render\-btrbk\-conf"
into a temporary file, and
.B "btrbk dryrun"
reads that file: the btrbk.conf the real run would have written by then, so a
pending retirement or adoption is previewed as it will happen (the target
directory a new adopted source sends to is created and logged, as the real
run does).
.PP
Prints a
.B "SUBVOLUME SYNC"
report: what was adopted, retired or revived, every exclusion with its
pattern, how many subvolumes inside snapshot trees were skipped on each volume,
whether btrbk.conf was regenerated, and any subvolume that could not be placed.
If the system clock reads before 2026\-01\-01, no retirement is stamped: the
report says so and the sync fails.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol sync
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run
.RB [ \-\-render\-btrbk\-conf
.IR PATH ]]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and write nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-render\-btrbk\-conf " PATH"
Only with
.BR \-\-dry\-run :
write the btrbk.conf the real run would leave in place into
.IR PATH ,
which must already exist as a regular file (it is never created, and a
symlink is refused). config.toml and the real btrbk.conf are not touched.
.PP
Exit status:
.B 0
sync ran and nothing failed;
.B 1
something failed (a volume was not read, a subvolume could not be placed,
the files could not be written, a retirement was refused because of the
clock, or there was no btrbk.conf to render);
.B 2
the configuration could not be loaded.
.\"
.SS subvol expire
Delete the backups of retired subvolumes whose window has passed. Run by
.B backup\-run.sh
directly after btrbk, while the targets are mounted.
.PP
A retired entry's snapshots are looked for in each
.I location:
every target directory its source sends to, and the source's own snapshot
directory. On a target they are kept
until the retirement date plus that target's longest retention window (daily
x1, weekly x7, monthly x31, yearly x366 days), then all deleted from that
target together; until then the report reads
.RI \(dq N " snapshots kept, deleted on or after " YYYY\-MM\-DD \(dq.
On the source side they are kept until the retirement date
plus the shortest of the targets' windows, then deleted. A target with no
retention configured keeps them and says
so. Expiry mounts nothing; a location whose mount point is not mounted is
reported as not reachable and left alone. Nothing is deleted from a location that is not a mountpoint or cannot be
read completely, that is also used by a live entry of the same series name,
or that holds names resembling the series that are not recognised btrbk
snapshots; nor at a location whose newest snapshot of the series is dated
more than a day after the retirement date (that date is wrong). Nothing of an
entry is deleted while a path with its name exists on its mounted source
volume, and nothing at all while the system clock reads before 2026\-01\-01
(reported, exit 1). A clock that wrongly reads far in the future at expiry time
is not detected and would expire retired series early; live subvolumes are
never touched by expiry. Run by hand, it keeps every retired entry whose source
volume is not mounted. When the run's sync failed,
.B backup\-run.sh
runs this command with
.B \-\-dry\-run
only. The entry leaves config.toml only once nothing remains to expire
(never if it is the last entry, or its source sends to no target, and never
on a dry run); a source left with no entries by that leaves with it. The
search index is pruned of the deleted snapshots.
.PP
Prints a
.B "RETIRED SUBVOLUMES"
report, or nothing when no entry is retired.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol expire
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to the SQLite index database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be deleted and delete nothing.
.PP
Exit status:
.B 0
ran and nothing failed;
.B 1
something failed, including deleted snapshots that could not be removed from
the index;
.B 2
the configuration could not be loaded.
.\"
.SS health
Show backup system health \(em drive status, SMART diagnostics,
disk usage, and growth trends.
.PP
With a
.B "role = \(dqmirror\(dq"
target configured it also prints a
.B "Recovery OS"
line per mirror target: read live from
.I <mount>/@
while the target is mounted, otherwise the record the last backup run kept in
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/recovery\-os.json ,
with the time it was taken and the age recomputed for today. Run it as root
while the drives are mounted, or the live read reports the files it may not
read without updating their access times; each line ends with the number of
paths that could not be read, when there are any. A stale, unreadable
or never\-checked recovery OS is also a warning, so the status reads
.BR WARNING .
.PP
.B btrdasd health
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS recovery\-os inspect
Read one recovery OS root and print its
.B "RECOVERY OS"
block. Every
.B "role = \(dqmirror\(dq"
target carries its own bootable install under
.I @
on the filesystem that receives the backups; this reads it and never writes
to it. Read: the name in
.IR etc/os\-release ,
the kernels under
.IR usr/lib/modules ,
the install date and the dates of the last full system upgrade attempted
and the last one applied from
.I var/log/pacman.log
(the install date is that of the log's first line \(em pacman's first entry
on an install is its
.B pacman \-b
from the live ISO \(em and is unknown when that line carries no date; an
attempt is a
.RB \(dq "starting full system upgrade" \(dq
line, logged before anything is downloaded or verified; it was applied only if
.RB \(dq "[ALPM] transaction completed" \(dq
follows before the next pacman invocation starts),
and the installed versions of btrfs\-progs, btrbk, linux\-cachyos and
das\-backup\-manager from
.I var/lib/pacman/local
(matched by the package's own name field). Not even an access time is
written: files and directories are opened with
.BR O_NOATIME ,
which needs root or ownership of the file (without it the item is reported
as unreadable rather than read), and no symlink inside the root is followed,
since following one updates the link's access time. So
.I etc/os\-release
is read from
.IR usr/lib/os\-release ,
the fallback
.BR os\-release (5)
names, when it is a link. A root that is itself a symlink is refused, and a
file that is not a regular file is not opened.
Whatever is there but cannot be read prints as
.B unknown
and is listed under
.BR "Could not read" ;
whatever is not there at all is said to be absent. A package missing from a
readable database prints as
.BR "not installed" .
.PP
The OS is
.B STALE
when its last APPLIED full upgrade is more than
.B [recovery_os].max_age_days
old, or is dated after today. When no full upgrade was ever applied, the age
is counted from the install date against the same limit, and the reason reads
.RB \(dq "never upgraded since install on" " DATE " ( "N days" )\(dq;
with neither date known it is
.B STALE
and no age is shown. The
.B Age
row says which date it counts from
.RB ( "since last applied upgrade" " or " "since install" ),
and the
.B Installed
row is shown for every drive. It is also
.B STALE
when a later attempt did not
complete (shown as
.BR "Last attempt" ,
with both dates in the reason \(em typically an expired keyring; on an
install never upgraded the reason reads
.RB \(dq "install" " DATE" ", never completed an upgrade; last attempt" " DATE " "did not complete" \(dq);
when its
newest kernel's major.minor series is lower than the host's running kernel,
or either kernel is unknown; or when its btrfs\-progs is older than the
host's. The btrfs\-progs versions are compared only when both are plain dotted
numbers (pkgrel ignored); otherwise the row says
.B not compared
and gives no verdict. The reason says whether an unknown item was absent or
could not be read. An unknown age is never shown as a number.
.PP
.B btrdasd recovery\-os inspect
.B \-\-root
.I PATH
.B \-\-label
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.TP
.BI \-\-root " PATH"
The OS root, e.g.
.IR /mnt/backup\-system\-recovery\-A/@ .
.TP
.BI \-\-label " NAME"
Name to print for the drive.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, read for
.BR [recovery_os].max_age_days .
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object:
.BR label ,
.BR root ,
.B status
.RB ( current ,
.BR stale ,
.BR unreadable ),
.BR error ,
.B os
and
.B assessment
(with
.BR os.installed ,
.B assessment.age_days
and
.BR assessment.age_basis :
.BR last_applied_upgrade ,
.BR install ,
or null when there is no age).
.PP
Exit status:
.B 0
current;
.B 1
stale;
.B 2
the root is not a directory, or the configuration could not be loaded.
.\"
.SS recovery\-os status
Print the
.B "RECOVERY OS"
report section for every
.B "role = \(dqmirror\(dq"
target, in config order: each one that is mounted at its configured mount
point is read as for
.BR "recovery\-os inspect" ,
with
.I <mount>/@
as the root; the others print
.BR "not mounted" .
.B backup\-run.sh
runs it after btrbk, while the targets are mounted, and records the result as
an operation: exit 0 is OK (the detail names any mirror that was not mounted),
exit 1 is WARN (the report status reads
.BR "COMPLETED WITH WARNINGS" ,
the run is still recorded as a success), anything else is FAIL. The script
bounds the command with
.B timeout
(300 s, then KILL after 10 s more); a check that times out is a FAIL, and the
run goes on to unmount and report.
.PP
.B btrdasd recovery\-os status
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-state\-file
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-file " PATH"
Record what was read from each drive in
.I PATH
(JSON, replaced atomically, mode 644) for
.BR "btrdasd health" .
A drive that is not mounted keeps its earlier record. An existing file that
cannot be read or parsed, or that is not record schema version 2 (version 2
added the install date), is left as it is and the command exits 2, its error
naming the file, the version found and the command that clears it; every
later run fails the same way until it is removed.
.B btrdasd health
reports such a record as unreadable.
.B backup\-run.sh
passes
.B $DAS_RECOVERY_OS_STATE
(default
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/recovery\-os.json ),
except on a dry run.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print a JSON array of the per\-drive objects described under
.BR "recovery\-os inspect" ,
with
.B status
also
.BR not_mounted .
.PP
Exit status:
.B 0
every mounted recovery OS is current, or none is mounted;
.B 1
at least one is stale;
.B 2
a mounted target has no readable
.IR @ ,
the state file could not be written, or the configuration could not be loaded.
.\"
.SS recovery\-os hold\-disk
An internal helper, not listed by
.BR \-\-help :
.B recovery\-os\-vm.sh
starts it for the length of a session in which a recovery drive is lent to
the
.B recovery\-os\-updater
virtual machine. It opens the whole disk read\-only with
.BR O_EXCL ,
an exclusive claim on the block device. While the claim stands, the kernel
refuses to mount any partition of that disk on the host \(em by device or by
UUID, in any mount namespace \(em while the virtual machine's own
non\-exclusive open of the disk still works. Two kernels mounting one BTRFS
filesystem corrupt it; this is what keeps the host's out. Conversely, a disk
with any partition mounted cannot be claimed, so a hold that succeeds also
proves that nothing on the host has the drive mounted.
.PP
Once the claim is in place it prints one line,
.IR "held PATH pid PID" ,
flushed, and then waits for
.BR SIGTERM ,
.B SIGINT
or
.B SIGHUP
\(em blocked first, then given back their default disposition, so a parent
that ignored one (a shell does SIGINT for every background job) cannot make
it deaf to it. On one of them it closes the disk \(em the claim ends \(em says so on
standard error and exits 0. It never reads standard input and never writes
standard output again, so it does not end when the process that started it
dies or closes its pipes. Ctrl\-C or a hangup in a terminal does reach it,
though, unless it was started in a session of its own; the script starts it
with
.BR setsid (1),
in a transient systemd scope of its own
.RB ( "systemd\-run \-\-scope" ,
.IR das\-recovery\-os\-holder\- LABEL .scope ,
outside the operator's login session, which can end without ending it), and
lets it inherit its descriptor of
.IR /run/das\-maintenance.lock ,
so that the claim and the lock both outlive the script while the virtual
machine may still be running. The lock's record then names this process, the
one that holds it. A
.B SIGKILL
ends the claim too: the kernel drops it with the descriptor.
.PP
The claim says nothing about whether the recovery OS boots. When the virtual
machine's firmware tries a network (PXE) boot or stops in its UEFI shell, the
disk did not boot; the firmware can be pointed at the loader on the drive's
ESP by hand, as
.I docs/DISASTER\-RECOVERY\-GUIDE.md
("In the recovery\-os\-updater VM") describes.
.PP
.B btrdasd recovery\-os hold\-disk
.B \-\-device
.I PATH
.TP
.BI \-\-device " PATH"
Any block device (a symbolic link is followed); anything else is refused. It
does not check that the device is a whole disk \(em
.B recovery\-os\-vm.sh
passes only a whole disk, the drive's
.I /dev/disk/by\-id/ata\-MODEL_SERIAL
link, and checks that itself.
.PP
.B \-\-json
is refused: the output is one plain line for a script to read.
.PP
Exit status:
.B 0
released on one of those signals;
.B 2
refused \(em the path is not a block device (it is never opened then: an
open of a FIFO would block), the disk is in use
.RB ( EBUSY :
mounted, or held by another program), any other open error, the device
behind the path changed while it was being opened, the hold could not be
announced on standard output, or
.BR \-\-json .
.\"
.SS scrub run
Run a full scrub pass now: acquire locks, mount each configured scrub
target by filesystem UUID, run
.BR "btrfs scrub start \-B" ,
parse the result, unmount, persist state, and email a report \(em the
same path the scheduled systemd timer uses. A filesystem whose last scrub was
interrupted, with nothing scrubbing it now, is resumed with
.B "btrfs scrub resume \-B"
instead, falling back to
.B "btrfs scrub start \-B \-f"
when there is nothing to resume. The targets are scrubbed one at a time, in
.B [scrub].targets
order.
.PP
If another scrub pass holds
.IR /run/das\-scrub.lock ,
this one is skipped (exit 0). The shared maintenance lock is waited for, so a
pass started during a backup begins when the backup ends.
.PP
This runs even when
.B [scrub].enabled
is
.B false
in config.toml: that flag only gates whether the
.I scheduled
timer fires, never a direct invocation of this command. A manual run is
exactly the intended use of a temporarily-disabled schedule, and a
warning is printed to make that explicit rather than silent.
.PP
.B Exit code:
0 means the pass
.I ran
(scrubbing was attempted on at least one target), regardless of what it
found \(em per-FS errors, aborted scrubs, and unmount problems are
reported via the FAILURE email and
.B "btrdasd health"
Critical escalation, never via this exit code. Nonzero means the pass
could
.I not
run at all: a config load failure, a lock-file I/O error, or every
target failing before any scrub was attempted. This split (bd
.BR DAS\-Backup\-Manager\-18p )
exists so a Sentinel-monitored systemd unit never retry-loops a real
multi-hour scrub over failing hardware just because it found damage
\(em only a genuine can't-even-start failure, which fails in seconds,
trips the unit into
.B failed
state.
.PP
.B btrdasd scrub run
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS scrub status
Show the last scrub result for every configured scrub target.
.PP
Resolved by filesystem UUID, never by mount path, so this works while
the DAS filesystems are unmounted. Reads the engine's persisted state
.RI ( /var/lib/das\-backup/scrub\-state.json )
when an entry exists, falling back to the raw btrfs record
.RI ( /var/lib/btrfs/scrub.status. <fsuuid> )
for a filesystem with scrub history predating this CLI or the state
file. A target with neither source is reported as
.BR "NEVER SCRUBBED" ,
not an error.
.PP
.B btrdasd scrub status
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS scrub cancel
Cancel the filesystem currently being scrubbed.
.PP
Manual operator action only \(em the scrub interlock never cancels a
pass automatically. Locates the actively-scrubbing filesystem via the
engine's lock and live kernel state, then issues
.B "btrfs scrub cancel"
against it. A no-op (clean exit, no error) when no scrub is running. Exits 1
if the cancel fails.
.PP
.B btrdasd scrub cancel
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS doctor
Subvolume drift detector \(em find source subvolumes that exist on disk but
aren't backed up, and configured subvolumes that no longer exist on disk.
.PP
For each configured
.I [[source]]
volume (deduplicated \(em multiple sources commonly share one physical
volume), mounts it if needed, runs
.BR "btrfs subvolume list" ,
and compares the result against every
.I [[source]].subvolume
name configured for that volume. The listing is the one
.B "subvol sync"
uses: a volume is listed only if every device its sources name is a real
mountpoint holding the expected filesystem UUID at its top level, and an empty
listing counts as a failure. Built-in exclusions (never configurable)
skip anything under a
.I .snapshots/
or
.I .btrbk\-snapshots/
directory at any depth, and the
.B @tmp
/
.B @var\-tmp
ephemeral subvolumes. The
.I [subvolumes].exclude
list, and the older
.I [doctor].exclude
(both shell-style glob patterns
.RI ( * / ? ),
merged), add further exclusions on top; the same rules as
.BR "subvol sync" ,
including that a pattern names a subvolume without a trailing slash and covers
everything nested under it.
.PP
Missing subvolumes (on disk, not configured by a live entry, not excluded)
are reported in one section,
.BR "NOT BACKED UP" ,
because the backup run adopts new subvolumes itself
.RB ( "subvol sync" ):
a finding means that step failed or has not run since the subvolume appeared,
and the report advises
.BR "btrdasd subvol sync \-\-dry\-run" .
A retired entry is neither configured nor stale. Stale config entries
(configured, not retired, no longer on disk) are reported separately.
.PP
.B Locking:
non-blocking only \(em a drift check that finds either its own singleton lock
.RI ( /run/das\-doctor.lock )
or the shared maintenance lock
.RI ( /run/das\-maintenance.lock ,
held by a running backup or scrub) already taken defers immediately rather
than waiting, since a fast read-mostly scan has nothing urgent enough to
delay a real backup or scrub for.
.PP
.B Exit code:
.B 0
means no drift was found, or the check deferred because a lock was held
(never treated as a failure).
.B 1
means drift was found (missing or stale subvolumes). Unlike
.BR "btrdasd scrub run" ,
this genuinely surfaces through the exit code, because a drift check has no
Sentinel multi-hour-retry hazard to protect against.
.B 2
means the check could not run at all: a config load failure, a lock I/O
error, or every configured source volume failed to mount or list (nothing
was ever examined).
.B 3
means at least one configured volume failed to mount or list while others
were checked (that volume's subvolumes went unchecked), or a volume the check
mounted could not be unmounted again (listed under
.BR "LEFT MOUNTED" ).
It outranks 1: an incomplete check cannot claim its drift list is complete.
.PP
.B btrdasd doctor
.RB [ \-\-check\-drift ]
.RB [ \-\-email ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.B \-\-check\-drift
Run the subvolume drift check. Currently the only check this command
performs \(em the flag exists so future checks can be selected individually
without a breaking CLI change. Omitting it still runs the drift check today.
.TP
.B \-\-email
Email a report via the configured SMTP settings, but only when drift or an
error was found \(em a clean run stays silent even with this flag, so the
weekly timer doesn't send an all-clear every Sunday.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS completions
Generate shell completions.
.PP
.B btrdasd completions
.I SHELL
.TP
.I SHELL
Shell to generate completions for. One of
.BR bash ,
.BR elvish ,
.BR fish ,
.BR powershell ,
or
.BR zsh .
.\"
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Produce machine-readable JSON output on all read commands.
This flag is global and may be placed before any subcommand.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit. The version printed is the Rust crate's
three-part version (for example
.B 0.7.22
for release 0.7.22.3).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful operation. Also: a command that found its lock held and deferred or
declined
.RB ( backup ,
.BR reconcile ,
.BR reindex ,
.BR forget ,
.BR purge ,
.BR doctor ,
.BR "scrub run" ),
and a scrub pass that ran, whatever it found.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (backup failure, validation error, missing configuration, a
mount point the command mounted and could not unmount again, etc.).
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed. Also used by
.B forget
(no readable btrbk.conf),
.B "subvol sync"
and
.B "subvol expire"
(configuration could not be loaded),
.B recovery\-os
(could not be checked; 1 there means a recovery OS is stale), and
.B doctor
(could not run).
.TP
.B 3
.B "btrdasd doctor"
only: the check ran but some volume went unexamined; see its section.
.TP
.B 75
.B walk
or a
.B restore
command run with
.B \-\-no\-wait
found the DAS maintenance lock held and deferred: nothing was mounted or
changed. (EX_TEMPFAIL: try again later.)
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
Editor for
.BR "config edit" .
Default:
.BR vi .
.TP
.BR DAS_REPORT_TO ", " DAS_REPORT_FROM
Override the report recipient and sender address
.RB ( [email].to ,
.BR [email].from )
for the emails this program sends. Meant for testing: the sender selects the
mail relay's upstream credential.
.TP
.BR DAS_SCRUB_STATE ", " DAS_BTRFS_STATUS_DIR
Override the scrub state file and the directory of btrfs scrub status
records. Meant for tests only.
.TP
.B DAS_MAINTENANCE_LOCK_FD
The number of an open descriptor on
.I /run/das\-maintenance.lock
through which the calling process holds the lock.
.B walk
and the
.B restore
commands then use that hold instead of waiting for the lock, which would never
end: the caller holds it until the command finishes.
.B backup\-run.sh
sets it for the
.B walk
it runs. It is checked, not trusted: the descriptor must be that file, the lock
must be held, and it must be held through that descriptor \(em or the command
fails. A descriptor on a free lock is refused rather than allowed to take it.
If the real holder lets go at the very moment of the check, a descriptor that
held nothing may still end up holding the lock: its process then holds it,
with no holder recorded, until it exits.
.TP
.B DAS_RECOVERY_OS_STATE
Override the recovery OS record
.B btrdasd health
reads, and the one
.B backup\-run.sh
writes. Meant for tests only.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a full backup pipeline:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd backup run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview a backup without making changes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd backup run \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a full (non-incremental) backup of specific sources to specific targets
(labels as configured in config.toml):
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd backup run \-\-full \-\-sources nvme,ssd \-\-targets primary\-22tb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview which subvolumes the next backup would adopt or retire:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd subvol sync \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview what expiry of retired subvolumes would delete (targets must be
mounted):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd subvol expire \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search for files matching a prefix across all indexed snapshots:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd search 'report*'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search with JSON output and limited results:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd \-\-json search 'config' \-\-limit 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Browse a snapshot directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd restore browse /mnt/backup\-22tb/nvme/home.20260228T0300
.fi
.RE
.PP
Restore specific files from a snapshot (the destination must be under
.BR [restore].allowed_roots ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd restore file /mnt/backup\-22tb/nvme/home.20260228T0300 /tmp/restored user/.bashrc user/.config/kdeglobals
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show drive health and backup status:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd health
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show backup schedule and next run times:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd schedule show
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show the last scrub result for every configured DAS filesystem:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd scrub status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Cancel a scrub currently in progress:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd scrub cancel
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check for subvolume drift (source vs config) and email a report if found:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd doctor \-\-check\-drift \-\-email
.fi
.RE
.PP
List all configured subvolumes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd subvol list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Index snapshots on a backup target:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd walk /mnt/backup\-22tb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run the interactive setup wizard:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd setup
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check the installation, including whether the targets are hidden from udisks
and which targets lack a
.BR mount_uuid :
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd setup \-\-check
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non-interactive full uninstall (preserves the backup database):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd setup \-\-uninstall\-all \-\-force
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non-interactive reinstall from existing config:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd setup \-\-force
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db
SQLite database with FTS5 content index and backup history.
.TP
.I /etc/das\-backup/config.toml
Main configuration file for sources, targets, schedule, and email settings.
.TP
.I /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf
btrbk configuration file, generated from config.toml by
.BR "btrdasd setup" ,
.B "subvol sync"
(every backup run), the
.B subvol
commands that change an entry, and the GUI. Do not edit it by hand.
.TP
.I /etc/das\-backup/.manifest
The files
.B btrdasd setup
generated, one per line; read by
.B \-\-check
and
.BR \-\-uninstall .
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/99\-das\-backup\-udisks\-ignore.rules
Generated udev rule that hides the backup targets from udisks, matched on the
targets' drive serials and
.BR mount_uuid .
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/growth.log
Disk usage growth log for trend monitoring, appended by
.B backup\-run.sh
and read by
.BR "btrdasd health" .
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/last\-report.txt
The last backup report, written before it is emailed
.RB ( [general].last_report ).
.TP
.IR /run/das\-backup.lock ", " /run/das\-scrub.lock ", " /run/das\-reconcile.lock ", " /run/das\-doctor.lock
Singleton locks: one backup, scrub, reconcile pass (also taken by
.B reindex
and
.BR forget / purge )
or drift check at a time.
.TP
.I /run/das\-maintenance.lock
Shared maintenance lock held by every job that mounts the backup targets \(em
backups, scrubs,
.BR walk ,
.BR restore ,
.BR reconcile ,
.BR reindex ,
.BR forget ,
.BR purge ,
the drift check, and the GUI's index and restore jobs \(em so they never
overlap. Backups, scrubs,
.B walk
and
.B restore
wait for it (a GUI job stops waiting when cancelled); the others defer.
Whoever holds it writes one line into it naming itself, such as
.BR "btrdasd restore browse pid 4242" ,
and empties it again on release.
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/scrub\-state.json
Persisted scrub state, keyed by filesystem UUID (mode 644). Absent until
the first
.B btrdasd scrub run
completes.
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/recovery\-os.json
What the last backup run read from each recovery drive's own OS, keyed by
target label (mode 644). Written by
.BR "recovery\-os status \-\-state\-file" ,
read by
.B "btrdasd health"
and by
.BR recovery\-os\-vm.sh ,
which starts a session only when the drive's entry says btrbk will not run
when its OS boots.
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/recovery\-os\-vm\-sessions
When
.B recovery\-os\-vm.sh
last booted each recovery drive's OS in the virtual machine, one
.I "LABEL SECONDS"
line per target label (mode 644): written before the OS boots and again when
the disk is given back. A boot record made before that time no longer counts.
.TP
.I /run/das\-recovery\-os\-vm/
Kept by
.B recovery\-os\-vm.sh
while a recovery drive is lent to the
.B recovery\-os\-updater
virtual machine (mode 700): per target label, the
.B "recovery\-os hold\-disk"
process's pid, the disk it holds and its scope
.RI ( LABEL .holder),
that process's output, and the disk definition attached. Removed when the
disk is given back.
.TP
.I /var/lib/btrfs/scrub.status.<fsuuid>
Raw btrfs scrub status record for one filesystem, written by btrfs\-progs.
Read directly by
.B btrdasd scrub status
as a fallback when no state-file entry exists.
